Done. `scorer_out.npz` and `NOTES.md` are written in the step3 directory.

**→ Result.** The evidence set goes from 16/32 to 32/32 correct (from `sanity.py`). The step-1 and step-2 units are embedded byte-for-byte unchanged, and the original scorer weights are untouched.

**→ What I changed.** Two new units on the account_security option, appended to the extra ReLU module. Each is an AND of a content score and an option gate, the same construction as step 2. Unit A fires on general eligibility content (age limits, supported countries, residency). Unit B fires on card-phrased eligibility ("can I get a card if I live in the US"), built from card-only subsets so the "card" feature cancels out.

**→ How the directions were made.** Variance-normalized mean differences of marker vectors from 834 eligibility and 1854 contrast messages I wrote, in a label-free PC basis. No fitting to evidence labels. The evidence set was only a held-out check.

**→ Held-out checks (from `make5.py`).** Hand-written eligibility: 52/54 route to account_security. PIN sets: 0/470 changed. Exchange-rate sets: 0/64 changed. Card-abroad and card-delivery contrast: 0/200 changed. About 8 flips among roughly 2,500 other contrast messages, mostly "do you support dollars" style wording.

**→ Mistake caught mid-task.** My first direction also fired on blocked-PIN messages and would have broken correction 2. Guard: the build script now runs a regression check against every step-1 and step-2 message set before anything is written, and the step-2 PIN set is part of the contrast set.

**Also found:** two hand-written cases still route to cards ("I live overseas, can I still use the app?", "Am I allowed to use this from the Philippines?"); the content signal for "use this/the app" plus residency is weak.
